course: dictionary
lesson: 5757
title: Not every squiggle is an English word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some sounds look like English words but belong to other languages. «Lu» is one of them. It is a piece of Mandarin pinyin, not an English word, so you will not find it in an English story as a word with its own meaning.
explain: Context tells you where you are. In a Mandarin lesson, «lù» means road or way, as in «yi lu ping an», which wishes someone a safe journey. Look at the words around it and you can tell the language. Ask yourself: is this English? If not, do not guess an English meaning.
explain: Watch out for the trap. In British English, «loo» is an informal word for toilet, and it sounds just like «lu». The same sound can mean different things, so read the whole phrase before you decide.
fact: «Lu» is not an English word.
fact: In Mandarin, «lù» means road or way, as in «yi lu ping an», have a safe journey.
fact: In Mandarin, «lǜ» means green, as in «lǜ sè», green colour.
fact: «Loo» is an informal British English word for toilet, and it sounds like «lu».
fact: «Salut!» is said between friends, children and family to mean hi or bye. It sounds too cheeky with a teacher or shopkeeper.
exercise: Your friend says «yi lu ping an» in a Mandarin lesson. What does «lù» mean here? | A short form of a name | *Road or way | Toilet | Green colour
exercise: A British friend says, «I need the loo.» What does loo mean? | *Toilet | Green | A Mandarin lesson | Road
exercise: Which word means green in Mandarin? | lù | loo | *lǜ | ping
test: Is «lu» an English word you can use in English writing? | Yes, it means toilet | Yes, it means road | *No, it is a piece of Mandarin pinyin | Yes, it means green
test: Why would «Salut!» sound cheeky to a teacher? | It means goodbye forever | *It is too informal for someone you should show respect to | It is the Mandarin word for road | It is a formal greeting in a letter
exam: A story uses «Lu» and seems to mean toilet. What should you check first? | *Read the words around it to work out the language and meaning | Always choose the English meaning, whatever the sentence says | Look for a new name | Skip the whole story
